sections.body_md:
Section 48 of the **Indian Partnership Act, 1932** governs the settlement of accounts between partners after dissolution, subject to any agreement among them.

**(a) Losses**, including deficiencies of capital: paid first out of **profits**, next out of **capital**, and lastly by the **partners individually** in their profit-sharing ratio.

**(b) Application of assets** (including sums partners contribute to make up deficiencies):
1. debts of the firm to **third parties**;
2. each partner rateably what is due for **advances (loans)**, as distinct from capital;
3. each partner rateably what is due on account of **capital**;
4. the **residue**, if any, among the partners in their profit-sharing ratio.

**Working:** loss = total claims (outside debts + partners' loans + capitals) − cash available; capital paid = capital − share of loss.

answer.model_answer_md:
**(a) Losses**, including deficiencies of capital, are paid first out of profits, next out of capital and lastly, if necessary, by the partners individually in the proportions in which they were entitled to share profits.

**(b) Assets** of the firm, including any sums contributed by partners to make up deficiencies of capital, are applied in this order:
1. in paying the debts of the firm to third parties;
2. in paying to each partner rateably what is due to him from the firm for advances (loans) as distinguished from capital;
3. in paying to each partner rateably what is due to him on account of capital;
4. the residue, if any, is divided among the partners in the proportions in which they were entitled to share profits.
